%S A402268 30,70,84,105,132,140,154,165,168,182,260,273,280,306,357,364,380,385,
%T A402268 399,418,420,455,495,520,546,552,560,585,595,598,616,644,646,663,690,
%U A402268 741,756,759,765,770,782,805,840,858,870,897,900,918,1015,1044,1054,1080
%N A402268 Composite numbers k such that min_{1<j<=k/2} gcd(k, binomial(k,j)) > sqrt(k).
%C A402268 Let f(k) = min_{1<j<=k/2} gcd(k, binomial(k,j)). For k = p^2 with p prime, f(k) = p = sqrt(k), so f(k) >= sqrt(k) holds for infinitely many composite k. This sequence therefore requires the strict inequality f(k) > sqrt(k).
%C A402268 Erdős and Szekeres gave the examples f(30) = 6, f(70) = 10 and f(154) = 14 (the value f(70) = 0 printed in their paper is a misprint). They wrote that there are probably infinitely many such k, but that they could not prove it (Erdős Problem 700, part (b)). The data suggest that the sequence is infinite.
%C A402268 There are 48 terms <= 10^3, 420 terms <= 10^4, and 3190 terms <= 10^5.
%H A402268 Hassane Bakkaoui, <a href="/A402268/b402268.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..3190</a>
%H A402268 Thomas F. Bloom, <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/700">Erdős Problem 700</a>, Erdős Problems.
%H A402268 Paul Erdős and George Szekeres, <a href="https://www.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1978-46.pdf">Some number theoretic problems on binomial coefficients</a>, Austral. Math. Soc. Gaz. 5 (1978), 97-99.
%e A402268 30 is a term: the minimum is gcd(30, binomial(30,5)) = gcd(30, 142506) = 6 > sqrt(30) = 5.477...
%e A402268 210 is not a term: gcd(210, binomial(210,30)) = 14 < sqrt(210) = 14.491...
%o A402268 (PARI) f700(n) = vecmin(vector(n\2-1, j, gcd(n, binomial(n, j+1))));
%o A402268 isok(n) = n>3 && !isprime(n) && f700(n)^2 > n;
%o A402268 (Python)
%o A402268 from math import gcd, comb
%o A402268 from sympy import isprime
%o A402268 def f(n): return min(gcd(n, comb(n, j)) for j in range(2, n//2+1))
%o A402268 def ok(n): return n > 3 and not isprime(n) and f(n)**2 > n
%o A402268 print([n for n in range(1, 1201) if ok(n)])
